window.BOARD = {"rendered":"2026-11-27T14:58:00Z","prime_owner":"neve","types":["sketch","bug","enhancement","feature","research"],"areas":["board","config","ide","reed","fabric","selfreport","shuttle","burler","webster","stencil","loom","quarry","batten","shed","orch","creel","claude","codex","report","workspaces","deploy","dotnet","gate","testkit","pattern","shortcut","dobby","tender","hibernate","drift","darn","arbiter","landing","cleanup","termwindow","logger","git","cwd","friction","lenses","reconcile","preflight","parsers","process","fsutil","cli","pawl","lint","profiles","docs","heddle","tools","slack","weaver","testmap","desktop"],"sections":["Running","Ready","Layer A","Layer B","Layer C","Layer D","Layer E","Layer F","Layer G","Layer H","Draft","Research","Bugs","Enhancements","Features"],"projects":[{"handle":"P0","slug":"root","title":"Root: entries under no project","brief":"","labels":[],"parent":"","design_file":"","done":false},{"handle":"P2","slug":"borrowed-orchestrator-ideas","title":"Ideas borrowed from comparable agent orchestrators","brief":"","labels":["feature","burler","shed","shuttle","orch","report"],"parent":"P0","design_file":"design-borrowed-orchestrator-ideas.md","done":false},{"handle":"P3","slug":"intake-wave-2","title":"Intake wave 2: Describe text, infra stops, push retries","brief":"","labels":["enhancement"],"parent":"P0","design_file":"","done":false},{"handle":"P4","slug":"fabric-core","title":"Fabric split into a git-free core, a layout package and a thin git engine","brief":"","labels":["enhancement","fabric","testkit","pattern","cwd","loom","batten","landing","gate","orch","board","webster","drift","report","shed","stencil","heddle","ide","reconcile","docs"],"parent":"P0","design_file":"design-fabric-core.md","done":false},{"handle":"P5","slug":"intake-wave-3","title":"Intake wave 3: beam orch parent, scope and earlier checks","brief":"","labels":["enhancement"],"parent":"P0","design_file":"","done":false},{"handle":"P6","slug":"intake-motifs","title":"Intake motifs: run watch, landed records, closed area","brief":"","labels":["enhancement"],"parent":"P0","design_file":"","done":false},{"handle":"P7","slug":"closed-area","title":"A closed area per hub: Landlock and a seccomp connect supervisor","brief":"","labels":["feature","creel","cwd","reed","shuttle","claude","codex","dobby","slack","process","git","cli","docs","pattern","termwindow","hibernate","batten","ide","deploy"],"parent":"P0","design_file":"design-closed-area.md","done":false}],"entries":[{"handle":"T2","slug":"kde-window-placement-build","title":"Place lym's Konsole windows on KDE Plasma 6 desktops and tiling zones, remembered per hub","kind":"task","types":["feature"],"areas":["termwindow","desktop","reed","dobby","cwd","fabric","gate","pattern","docs"],"status":"running · Plan-Write","project":"P0","section":"Running","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"Filed from the Sketch `kde-window-placement`.\n\nTakes: termwindow's Launcher.OpenAttach and Resolve, desktophook's AfterWindow runner and config, dobbyengine's per-task lookup over hubgeom.TaskSettingChain, hubgeom.ParentOf, state.UpdateJSON, lock.AcquireWriteLock, standalonestate.UserStateDir, selfexec.LymBinary, runproc.Register, lymtemp, and the layout as fabric-core-foundation and whole-repo-guards-off-gates land it; the KWin facts Open risks lists, probed as the run's first step, each with a KWin script run through `qdbus6` and its console.log lines read from the journal: the read-only probes run first, the moving probes only once the operator runs or approves them, and a probe that contradicts a listed fact stops the run for the parent, with the probe's command and output, before any code rests on it\nDelivers: D1's pre-launch checks and --separate Konsole launch returning its pid; internal/kwinplace (new) with the script runner, script lock, query cache, kscreen reader, pure planner, places file and placement child; the hidden placement verb that lym reed window and lym machine start hand each window to; desktop.window_placement, desktop.tiling.min_tile_width and min_tile_height, and beam.window_placement; a cancellable window_command runner; the real-desktop contract test in a manual tier\nExports: kwinplace's placement entry and planner, termwindow's pid-returning launch and pre-launch check, dobbyengine.LookupWindowPlacement, the three desktop keys\n\nThe part changes these packages:\n\n- internal/kwinplace (about 2600 changed lines)\n- internal/termwindow (about 120 changed lines)\n- internal/desktophook (about 150 changed lines)\n- internal/reedcli (about 200 changed lines)\n- internal/machinecli (about 80 changed lines)\n- internal/beamsetting (about 30 changed lines)\n- internal/dobbyengine (about 100 changed lines)\n- internal/fabricengine (about 20 changed lines)\n- internal/lymdirs (about 10 changed lines)\n- internal/gateslot (about 20 changed lines)\n- cmd/lym (about 30 changed lines)\n- pattern (about 10 changed lines)\n- docs (about 20 changed lines)\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"tapestry","created":"2026-11-27T14:33:20Z","revised":"2026-11-27T14:33:20Z","design_file":"design-kde-window-placement-build.md","decision_file":"decision-kde-window-placement-build.md","revision":"281f90e820bf499a049d3d15a66d6d32ce98d9ec0fb2da0021fac6f372883018"},{"handle":"T4","slug":"shuttle-test-cut","title":"Cut shuttle and seat engines' tier1 test time at least in half","kind":"task","types":["enhancement"],"areas":["shuttle","claude","codex","testkit"],"status":"running · Darn-Write","project":"P0","section":"Running","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"## Aim (operator, 2026-11-27)\n\nCut the tier1 test time of internal/shuttleengine (with its claudeengine and codexengine subpackages) and internal/seatengine at least in half, measured before and after on this machine with `lym gate test --tier tier1` naming these packages.\nThe cut removes tests, it does not move them to another tier: delete tests that repeat what another test already pins, merge table cases that walk the same path, share heavy fixtures (repos, binaries, fake sessions) across a package's tests, and replace sleeps and real timeouts with injected clocks or shortened bounds.\nKeep one test for every behaviour a PATTERN or a criterion names.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Only the test files of internal/shuttleengine (with its claudeengine and codexengine subpackages) and internal/seatengine, plus test-only helpers they alone use.\n- No production change, except a seam a test needs to drop a sleep or real timeout, such as an injectable clock or bound.\n- Report the before and after times per package and the net test-line change in the change description.\n\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"darn","created":"2026-11-27T14:56:57Z","revised":"2026-11-27T14:56:57Z","design_file":"design-shuttle-test-cut.md","decision_file":"","revision":"9cd5751dad9bfb6d6a27a991cebae85c85f1e91e011326a6b670cadb53c0b8b3"},{"handle":"T5","slug":"orch-test-cut","title":"Cut orchcli and orchengine tier1 test time at least in half","kind":"task","types":["enhancement"],"areas":["orch","testkit"],"status":"running · Darn-Write","project":"P0","section":"Running","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"## Aim (operator, 2026-11-27)\n\nCut the tier1 test time of internal/orchcli and internal/orchengine at least in half, measured before and after on this machine with `lym gate test --tier tier1` naming these packages.\nThe cut removes tests, it does not move them to another tier: delete tests that repeat what another test already pins, merge table cases that walk the same path, share heavy fixtures (repos, binaries, fake sessions) across a package's tests, and replace sleeps and real timeouts with injected clocks or shortened bounds.\nKeep one test for every behaviour a PATTERN or a criterion names.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Only the test files of internal/orchcli and internal/orchengine, plus test-only helpers they alone use.\n- No production change, except a seam a test needs to drop a sleep or real timeout, such as an injectable clock or bound.\n- Report the before and after times per package and the net test-line change in the change description.\n\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"darn","created":"2026-11-27T14:56:57Z","revised":"2026-11-27T14:56:57Z","design_file":"design-orch-test-cut.md","decision_file":"","revision":"2b861b04fca5fd029d8412ad48470672df56576c8e0d5679cb4ffbfc77857d27"},{"handle":"T6","slug":"config-test-cut","title":"Cut config, configsync and hubreconcile tier1 test time in half","kind":"task","types":["enhancement"],"areas":["config","reconcile","testkit"],"status":"starting · Worktree-Create","project":"P0","section":"Running","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"## Aim (operator, 2026-11-27)\n\nCut the tier1 test time of internal/configengine, internal/configsync and internal/hubreconcile at least in half, measured before and after on this machine with `lym gate test --tier tier1` naming these packages.\nThe cut removes tests, it does not move them to another tier: delete tests that repeat what another test already pins, merge table cases that walk the same path, share heavy fixtures (repos, binaries, fake sessions) across a package's tests, and replace sleeps and real timeouts with injected clocks or shortened bounds.\nKeep one test for every behaviour a PATTERN or a criterion names.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Only the test files of internal/configengine, internal/configsync and internal/hubreconcile, plus test-only helpers they alone use.\n- No production change, except a seam a test needs to drop a sleep or real timeout, such as an injectable clock or bound.\n- Report the before and after times per package and the net test-line change in the change description.\n\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"darn","created":"2026-11-27T14:56:57Z","revised":"2026-11-27T14:56:57Z","design_file":"design-config-test-cut.md","decision_file":"","revision":"32339b20b4825d488b43a2dcbedcfcaac791cc82fd53b7affb0efeb85b9cd5c4"},{"handle":"T8","slug":"parsers-burler-test-cut","title":"Cut the parsers' and burlerengine's tier1 test time in half","kind":"task","types":["enhancement"],"areas":["parsers","burler","testkit"],"status":"halted · Worktree-Create","project":"P0","section":"Running","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"## Aim (operator, 2026-11-27)\n\nCut the tier1 test time of internal/discussionparser, internal/planparser and internal/burlerengine at least in half, measured before and after on this machine with `lym gate test --tier tier1` naming these packages.\nThe cut removes tests, it does not move them to another tier: delete tests that repeat what another test already pins, merge table cases that walk the same path, share heavy fixtures (repos, binaries, fake sessions) across a package's tests, and replace sleeps and real timeouts with injected clocks or shortened bounds.\nKeep one test for every behaviour a PATTERN or a criterion names.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Only the test files of internal/discussionparser, internal/planparser and internal/burlerengine, plus test-only helpers they alone use.\n- No production change, except a seam a test needs to drop a sleep or real timeout, such as an injectable clock or bound.\n- Report the before and after times per package and the net test-line change in the change description.\n\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"darn","created":"2026-11-27T14:56:58Z","revised":"2026-11-27T14:56:58Z","design_file":"design-parsers-burler-test-cut.md","decision_file":"","revision":"46564c690b32b28b9d37bea51b6efac23d077454a480df8db1c46653e1eeba7d"},{"handle":"T9","slug":"weaver-bench","title":"Bench Weaver against Webster on plans Webster already implemented: time, cost and quality","kind":"task","types":["research"],"areas":["weaver","webster"],"status":"running · Darn-Write","project":"P0","section":"Running","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"## Aim (operator, 2026-11-27)\n\nWeaver, the cold-start implementer with model escalation (`18abfaf50`), landed long ago and has never been tried on a real plan.\nThis bench runs it on plans Webster has already implemented and landed, and measures time, cost and quality against Webster on the same plans.\nIt is large, long and costly, and the operator judges it worth that.\n\n## Plans\n\n- Only existing plans that Webster implemented and that landed; no new plans are written.\n- Three plans, found in the records history (the plan directory and `_lym/webster/` state of landed tapestry runs), chosen so that:\n  - each is a Go plan of the current plan format, with 4 to 12 cards and at least two batches in Webster's partition;\n  - the three touch different packages;\n  - each one's base commit, the task branch's commit the plan was implemented on, can still be checked out and builds.\n- The report names each chosen plan, its landed commit and why it was chosen.\n\n## Runs\n\n- Each plan is implemented once by Weaver, from the plan's base commit, with the current lym and the hub's default settings: three runs.\n- One of the three plans, the one whose recorded Webster run is newest, is also implemented once by Webster under the same conditions, as a calibration: four runs in all (operator, 2026-11-27).\n  The calibration shows how far Webster's recorded numbers lie from today's lym, machine and load,\n  and the other two plans compare Weaver with their recorded Webster runs, adjusted by that calibration, with the adjustment stated.\n\n- Weaver uses its default ladder (`lym config weaver`); Webster its default engine and models.\n- Each run has a bench pair of its own, which the parent creates from this task's worktree when the run asks for it; this task's agent runs them.\n  The calibration plan's two runs run at the same time so they share the machine's load.\n- No run is repeated; a run lost to the machine (a reboot or an infrastructure stop) is rerun once and the report says so.\n\n## Measures\n\n- Wall time from the implementer row's start to its end, and the time in batches, gates and fixers.\n- Cost: tokens and dollars per model, from the sessions' usage records.\n- Weaver's ladder: how many attempts climbed past the first rung, and the stops that reached the stop chain.\n- Quality:\n  - whether the plan-level verify gate passes, and how many fixer rounds it took;\n  - the cards left undone or deviated from;\n  - a diff of each result against the landed commit, and the findings one Code-Review round (the hub's Burler reviewer, once per run) gives on each result.\n\n## Output\n\nA report under `docs/research/weaver/` with one table per measure across the four runs and the recorded Webster runs, the findings per run, and a recommendation:\nwhether Weaver can replace Webster as tapestry's implementer, and what it would need first.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"darn","created":"2026-11-27T10:28:28Z","revised":"2026-11-27T10:34:25Z","design_file":"design-weaver-bench.md","decision_file":"","revision":"719ff0ee4fa42597fd6a2eef14ea839e388a11528b6cba7955e7daf0eb530584"},{"handle":"T11","slug":"push-retry-not-halt","title":"Background pushes retry quietly; landing pushes and reads retry instead of failing","kind":"task","types":["bug"],"areas":["fabric","shed","landing","batten","dobby"],"status":"running · Plan-Burler-Review","project":"P3","section":"Running","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"## Decisions (operator, 2026-11-26)\n\n- No mail per failed push. lym retries quietly with backoff.\n  One summary notice goes to the orch when owed work has stayed unpushed past a threshold (30 minutes proposed), listing every branch still owed, and one notice when all is pushed again.\n- The reed status bar shows one field, `sync: \u003cminutes\u003e`: the minutes since this machine last had nothing owed to origin, so the operator sees before switching machines whether work is still only local.\n- Agents may still push, but a push of theirs that fails never stops them: their instructions say a failed push needs no action, since lym pushes their commits later, and no row or gate requires an agent's commits to be on origin.\n- lym pushes everything it owns through the one pusher with these rules: both branches of every pair, beam branches and the board. Branches the operator made outside lym are left alone.\n- How often lym pushes is config in seconds, adaptive: a short interval while pushes go through (60 s proposed), doubled after each failed push up to a ceiling (300 s to start with), and back to the short one after a push lands.\n  The reed field shows the interval in force beside the minutes since the last sync, such as `sync: 3m · 60s`.\n- A landing makes lym push at once, but a network outage never stops other work: other runs go on committing locally.\n  A landing that needs no GitHub call merges locally and hands its owed push to the pusher; one that needs GitHub, such as a pull request, waits for the network and retries, and never fails on a transient error.\n- A refusal that retrying cannot fix (rejected push, expired login, moved branch) stops at once with one notice.\n\n## Operator intent (2026-11-26)\n\nLoomYard stops too often because a push to origin fails, above all on VPN at work.\nlym and the agents only commit; lym pushes in the background when it suits and retries a failed push later, with fewer pushes and less traffic.\nA finished task is the exception: its landing is pushed at once, so a milestone never stays only local.\n\n## What `async-run-pusher` (`b4236816a`) already built\n\nRun transitions commit locally, and a per-worktree pusher started by each commit, the dobby's tick and a resume pushes both sides, retries owed pushes with backoff and posts one notice after a minute.\nRead first what each part below still needs on top of it:\n`lym fabric commit`'s and `lym board sync`'s own detached pushes, the synchronous landing pushes and GitHub reads, and Seed-IDE's check of the code tip on origin.\nA part the pusher already settles is named as such in the change description and dropped.\n\nBundled from the notes below; each part keeps its note's text.\n\n# Periodic async push of pairs whatever made the commits\n\nImported from [issue #247](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/247).\n\n## Idea\n\nAgents should only have to commit; they should never have to think about pushing.\nlym takes care of every push itself, asynchronously, without blocking an agent.\n\n## Seen\n\nlym already pushes asynchronously after `lym fabric commit`, but only then.\nAn agent that commits with plain `git commit` leaves its commits unpushed until some later lym step pushes the pair, and the push failures that follow land on whichever step happens to push next.\n\n## Proposal\n\nA periodic pusher in lym, for example in the dobby or its own small daemon, that pushes each live pair's code and records branches when they are ahead of origin, however the commits were made.\n\n- Periodic and not aggressive: a fixed interval of minutes, not a push per commit.\n- Never blocks an agent or a row; a failed push is retried at the next period, with backoff, and reported once rather than per attempt.\n- Rows that need origin current, such as Finalize, still push synchronously as now.\n\n\n# Transient push failures are mailed to the orch one by one instead of retried\n\nImported from [issue #238](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/238).\n\nOn a network that resets HTTPS connections now and then (git exits 128 with 'Recv failure: Connection reset by peer'), every failed background push sends the orch a creel message naming `lym fabric push` or `lym board sync` as the way forward. In one hour on one hub the orch got eight such messages, for the bolt branch main, task code branches and records branches, and each retry by hand succeeded at once or failed again only because the connection was still flaky.\n\nExpected: fabric retries a transport failure itself with backoff, and mails the orch only once retries are exhausted. One message then covers every branch still unpushed, rather than one message per push. The next pair push already carries a withheld code side, so a single summary would suffice.\n\nRelated: #193 (closed), which made runs retry transient fetch failures; pushes have no such retry.\n\n# Unreachable origin halts the run as failed repeatedly instead of one retryable stop\n\nImported from [issue #243](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/243).\n\nAn unreachable origin halted the run as `failed` with anomaly `producer-hard-failure` seven times in a row, across PR-Review and Finalize, instead of waiting or blocking once.\n\nWhat happened:\n- PR-Review and Finalize halted with `landingshed: ... push the task branch ... fabricengine: ls-remote of \u003ctask\u003e-weft ... Recv failure: Connection reset by peer`.\n- Finalize then halted three more times with `landingshed: Finalize: read who owns the run: board: origin is unreachable, so the owner write was not pushed: fetching origin for the board ... failed (gitrepo: ... git exited 128 ...)`.\n- One halt in between was `blocked` with anomaly `escalation-to-human`: `owner check: origin was unreachable, so who owns this run could not be judged; re-run \"lym loom resume\" in the task worktree once origin answers`.\n- Each halt wrote a note and a history entry; the history shows `Finalize / stuck` then `Finalize / goto` before each re-failure.\n\nWhy it is abnormal:\n- A transient network failure is classed as a hard failure, the same class as a producer defect, and the run state becomes `failed`.\n- The same condition surfaced under three different halt shapes (`failed`, `blocked`, `failed`), so the operator cannot tell one outage from three problems.\n- Each re-step re-ran immediately and failed again within about a minute, so the retries consumed attempts without waiting for the remote.\n\nWhat to do:\n- Classify unreachable-origin errors (ls-remote, fetch exit 128, connection reset) as one retryable or `blocked` kind with a single note, and have the driver wait or back off before re-stepping.\n- Keep `failed` for errors that retrying cannot fix.\n\nProvenance: task `retire-d9`; notes loom-halt-3.md, loom-halt-4.md, loom-halt-5.md, loom-halt-6.md, loom-halt-7.md, loom-halt-8.md, loom-halt-9.md.\n\n\n# Finalize halts as hard failure on a GitHub API EOF that lym marks transient\n\nImported from [issue #240](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/240).\n\nA `lym loom step` at the Finalize producer read a pull request's status from the GitHub API and got `unexpected EOF`.\nThe step was refused with `transient=github-api`, yet the run halted as `failed` with anomaly `producer-hard-failure`.\n\nQuoted from the trace:\n\n```\nlevel=WARN msg=\"shed: step refused\" kind=producer transient=github-api error=\"landingshed: Finalize: read the pull request's status: pullrequest: read pull request #54: Get \\\"https://api.github.com/repos/\u003cowner\u003e/\u003crepo\u003e/pulls/54\\\": unexpected EOF\"\n```\n\nHalt note text: `loom halted failed at Finalize`, `state: failed`, `anomaly: producer-hard-failure`, 15 history entries, all earlier steps done or skipped.\n\nThis is abnormal because lym itself labels the error transient, and a single dropped connection to the API then ends a run that had finished every earlier step.\nOther network failures in the same run (an unreachable origin before a push) were retried until they landed, so the Finalize read is inconsistent with them.\n\nSuggested fix: when a step is refused with a non-empty `transient` kind, retry it with backoff, or leave the run resumable, instead of recording `producer-hard-failure`.\nA failure of this kind should reach `failed` only after the retry budget is spent.\n\nProvenance: task `transient-tripout-overpull`, from `loom-halt-2.md`.\n\n\n# Seed-IDE fails the run when the task branch's detached push has not landed yet\n\nImported from [issue #218](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/218).\n\nImport note: the issue names files under `.lym/`, which is deleted with its worktree, so only what the issue quotes of them reached the board: `.lym/logs`.\n\n## What happened\n\nRun `weekly-time-report` (recipe raddle, promoted from a Sketch) stopped at Seed-IDE with:\n\n\u003e run \"weekly-time-report\" cannot act: the code tip e7b0cc2 the run's last transition names is not on origin yet; way forward: run \"lym batten run weekly-time-report\" again once the code push lands\n\nTimeline from `.lym/logs` (2026-11-24, CET):\n\n- 14:45:52 Worktree-Create done; the task branch forks from the prime's local `main` at e7b0cc2, one commit ahead of `origin/main`.\n- 14:45:52 and 14:45:55 fabricengine spawned detached pushes of the new pair (`fabric --warp-path …/weekly-time-report …`).\n- 14:45:57 Seed-IDE's bootstrap checked whether e7b0cc2 is on origin, found it was not, and refused the step.\n- Shortly after, the detached push landed: `git ls-remote origin` showed `refs/heads/weekly-time-report` at e7b0cc2.\n\nThe network was slow and resetting that day (`Recv failure: Connection reset by peer` on records pushes), so the detached push took longer than the 2–5 s gap.\n\n## Effect\n\n- The run stopped as failed, and the tackler handed it back to the parent asking whether to push the prime's `main`, which was not needed.\n- A plain `lym batten run weekly-time-report` afterwards went straight through Seed-IDE to Run-Shed.\n\n## Expected\n\nA check that the code tip is on origin, right after lym itself spawned the push that puts it there, should wait for that push, or retry for a while, instead of failing the run.\nThe stop should also say that lym's own push of the task branch is in flight, so the tackler does not ask the parent to push the parent branch.\n\n\n# Finalize blocks on untracked leftovers of a project the merge-in deleted\n\nImported from [issue #242](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/242).\n\n## Seen\n\nA darn run's Finalize merged its parent beam into the task worktree after its pull request had merged on GitHub.\nThe beam had just taken in a main commit that deleted a whole C# project.\nThe task worktree still held that project's directory with only untracked, gitignored content: `bin/`, `obj/` and generated fixtures.\nOnce git removed the tracked files, the directory was no longer ignored by the deleted `.gitignore`, so it showed as untracked.\nFinalize then blocked: \"the task worktree has uncommitted changes after the merge-in: …/NORCE.Import.CommonLibHarness/; commit or remove them on the task branch, then resume\".\n\nThe run had no live agent left to remove it.\nThe orch may not edit a task worktree, and the Go driver only asked the parent a question and stopped again on resume.\nThe only ways forward were a goto back to the writer, which would re-run review and PR rows for a merged pull request, or the operator removing the directory by hand.\n\n## Workaround\n\nThe operator removes the leftover directory by hand, then the orch runs `lym loom resume` in the task worktree.\n\n## Expected\n\nFinalize's merge-in treats a directory that holds only files the merge's base ignored, under a path the merge deleted, as leftover output:\nit removes it, or ignores it in the clean-tree check, and goes on.\nFailing that, the stop names a lym verb the parent can run to clear it, instead of needing a hand edit in the task worktree.\n\n\n\n# Seen 2026-11-26: a busy push lock fails a dobby start\n\nWith several tasks released at once, dobby's catch-up for `sandbox-closed-area-build` and `keep-landed-records` failed with `fabricengine: push lock busy: .../loomyard-weft/.weft/fabric.push.lock still held after 30s ... neither side was pushed`, and each needed a manual `lym batten run`.\nA held push lock is contention, not a failure: the catch-up should hand the owed push to the pusher and start the run.\n\n# Review findings carried over from `async-run-pusher` (`b4236816a`)\n\nCode-Review left these MEDIUM findings open; check each and close what is still open:\n\n- tests of the pusher's lock and start conditions (`internal/runpusher`);\n- tests of the pusher restart calls from the dobby tick and the batten and loom resumes (`internal/beamcli`, `internal/battencli`, `internal/loomcli`);\n- tests of the teardown's push and level wiring (`teardownPushSeams` in `internal/battencli/wire.go`);\n- Worktree-Teardown looping with pushes off (`internal/battenshed/teardown.go`).\n\n\n## From issue #254\n\nImported from [issue #254](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/254).\n\n## What happened\n\nA `lym shed` run halted `failed` with anomaly `interrupted-stop` at producer `Webster`.\nThe note says a machine stop likely ended the loop, which is not itself a defect.\nThe stderr it carries shows two things that are:\n\n1. The step wrote `status push is owed; retrying until it lands` for `producer=Drift-Expect` while origin was unreachable (`ls-remote` failed with `Connection reset by peer`).\n   It then logged `origin is unreachable before a push; pushing unchecked, so the push's own failure is recorded` twice, about 10 s apart.\n   The loop never produced an envelope, and the halt note was written only about 20 hours after the loop started, after the machine rebooted.\n   Nothing in the notes shows a bound on the retry, or any record of the push's own failure after the \"pushing unchecked\" lines.\n2. The note says step `Drift-Await` was in flight, while the stderr's producer is `Drift-Expect` and the history shows both `Drift-Await` and `Drift-Expect` as `skipped`.\n   The in-flight step name and the producer that owed the push disagree.\n\nQuoted lines (paths removed):\n\n```\nlevel=WARN msg=\"loomcli: status push is owed; retrying until it lands\" producer=Drift-Expect state=running error=\"fabricengine: push code side ...: ls-remote of \u003cbranch\u003e ...: exit 128: fatal: unable to access '\u003corigin\u003e': Recv failure: Connection reset by peer; way forward: run `lym fabric push` ... to retry the push\"\nlevel=WARN msg=\"loomcli: origin is unreachable before a push; pushing unchecked, so the push's own failure is recorded\"\nlevel=WARN msg=\"loomcli: origin is unreachable before a push; pushing unchecked, so the push's own failure is recorded\"\n```\n\n## Why it is abnormal\n\nA transient network failure on a status push should either land after a bounded retry or fail the step with a recorded push error.\nAn unbounded wait with no outcome stalls the whole run until something external kills it, and the halt then reads as a machine stop instead of a push problem.\n\n## What to do\n\n- Bound the \"push is owed\" retry, and record the push's final failure in the step's status and trace when it gives up.\n- Make the halt note's in-flight step and the producer owing the push name the same step, or say why they differ.\n\nProvenance: task `models-bit-rock-formation`, from `loom-halt-2.md`.\n\n# A rejected push can force origin past a commit made by hand on GitHub\n\nFolded from note B1 (board tidy, 2026-11-27).\n\n`pr-modes-no-agent` landed with an open point in its change description: a push can still drop a hand commit made on GitHub.\nFor a task pair, a rejected push is retried by `resetUnnamedTip` (`internal/fabricengine/pushclassified.go`),\nwhich can force-reset origin's code branch past the hand commit.\nThe change description names five other push paths that can push over such a commit.\nThis is read from the code and was not tested against a remote.\n\nA push that finds origin ahead with a commit lym did not make stops and names that commit, rather than resetting past it,\nas the decision above on refusals that retrying cannot fix says; check every push path the change description names.\n\n# Left open by `transient-stop-not-failed` (orch, 2026-11-27)\n\nThat task kept state `failed` for a step failure with a non-empty transient class and gave it its own anomaly kind, `AnomalyTransientStop` (`internal/loomengine/anomaly.go`);\nrecording it as a retryable stop is this task's (its Code-Review finding F1, accepted unconverged).\nIts finding F2 was also left open: the kind's doc comment, the `loomcli` package doc and the reflection template call every transient-stop halt one the driver re-steps,\nwhile the driver re-steps only `git-transport` and `github-api`; `agent-start` and `host-resources` get the loop's one re-step and then go to the tackler.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"tapestry","created":"2026-11-26T07:33:02Z","revised":"2026-11-27T11:16:53Z","design_file":"design-push-retry-not-halt.md","decision_file":"decision-push-retry-not-halt.md","revision":"bb1c72f7524d84d42d8e234437fdf114bae7bb2c65018a837b8ce1b91a310c60"},{"handle":"T1","slug":"loomcli-test-cut","title":"Cut loomcli's tier1 test time at least in half","kind":"task","types":["enhancement"],"areas":["loom","testkit"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Ready","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"## Aim (operator, 2026-11-27)\n\nCut the tier1 test time of internal/loomcli at least in half, measured before and after on this machine with `lym gate test --tier tier1` naming these packages.\nThe cut removes tests, it does not move them to another tier: delete tests that repeat what another test already pins, merge table cases that walk the same path, share heavy fixtures (repos, binaries, fake sessions) across a package's tests, and replace sleeps and real timeouts with injected clocks or shortened bounds.\nKeep one test for every behaviour a PATTERN or a criterion names.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Only the test files of internal/loomcli, plus test-only helpers they alone use.\n- No production change, except a seam a test needs to drop a sleep or real timeout, such as an injectable clock or bound.\n- Report the before and after times per package and the net test-line change in the change description.\n\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"darn","created":"2026-11-27T14:56:56Z","revised":"2026-11-27T14:56:56Z","design_file":"design-loomcli-test-cut.md","decision_file":"","revision":"7397a4da032e8056fc07dba4c704b6874467eb14f5bffb886e892fdc7ff070ae"},{"handle":"T12","slug":"beam-member-parent","title":"A beam member's run names its beam orch as parent, which may amend the beam's record","kind":"task","types":["bug"],"areas":["batten","landing","orch","stencil","shed","loom","pawl"],"status":"","project":"P5","section":"Layer A","waits_on":["T11"],"brief":"","body":"Bundled from the notes below; each part keeps its note's text.\n\n# A beam member's run records the prime orch as its parent orch\n\nImported from [issue #232](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/232).\n\n## Seen\n\nIn the DrillOpPlan hub on 2026-11-24, the run `models-stick-slip-map`, a member of the beam `models-torque-drag` and spawned from the beam's worktree, records the prime's orch `dop:orch` as its parent orch.\nIts PR-Review sent the `[pr-review request]` for PR #52 (into the beam branch `hanf/models-torque-drag`) to `dop:orch`, as ask 1 from the PR-Review session and as ask 2 from `dop:LOOM`.\nThe beam's orch `dop:models-torque-drag:orch`, which owns and released the run, reviewed the PR, and its answer was refused:\n\n```\ncaller dop:models-torque-drag:orch is neither the run's parent orch nor the strand the ask went to\n```\n\nThe beam's dobby sends its notices for the same run to `dop:orch` too.\nKnatte18/loomyard#231 shows the same gap in the tackler and driver prompts of a beam member.\n\n## Expected\n\nA beam member's run records the beam's orch as its parent orch, so its parent-review asks, notices and escalations go to the beam's orch and that orch's answer is accepted.\n\n## Workaround\n\nThe prime orch forwards each ask and notice to the beam's orch, and runs `lym loom parent-review answer \u003cslug\u003e --ask \u003cn\u003e --satisfied` in the task worktree on the beam orch's verdict.\n\n\n# Tackler and driver prompts of a beam member say no parent is recorded\n\nImported from [issue #231](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/231).\n\n## Seen\n\nIn the DrillOpPlan hub on 2026-11-10, the shed tackler of run `models-backend`, a member of the beam `adopt-models-sim`, got a prompt whose section \"Your parent\" read:\n\n```\nNo parent is recorded for this run.\nWhen you are blocked, write your report and stop.\n```\n\nThe run had a parent: the beam's orch `dop:adopt-models-sim:orch`, which owned and released it.\nOther tackler and driver prompts in the hub carried the same text, in the form \"No parent is recorded for the run, so the question is written for whoever owns it.\"\nThe tackler escalated on a merge conflict and stopped; the prime orch saw the stop and passed it to the beam orch by hand.\n\n## Expected\n\nThe tackler and driver prompts of a beam member name the beam's orch as the parent, so a blocked tackler or driver reaches it directly.\n\n## Workaround\n\nThe prime orch forwards a beam member's stop to the beam's orch by creel.\n\n\n# Beam orch cannot amend its own beam's decision record\n\nImported from [issue #236](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/236).\n\n## What happened\n\nIn hub `dop`, beam `models-torque-drag` (P1) holds a stored decision record.\nOn 2026-11-24 the operator made a decision that supersedes two of its decisions: a new member task maps the well's formation strength into the Models bit-rock law, where the beam record's D1 and D2 keep Models' bit-rock defaults.\nThe member's Discussion saw the conflict and asked the beam orch, which owns the beam record, to settle it.\n\nThe beam orch could not amend its own beam's record:\n\n- `lym loom decision add models-torque-drag --by parent ...` from the beam orch refused: `the caller \"dop:models-torque-drag:orch\" is a task session; way forward: a design call after the Discussion is the parent's to record; ask dop:orch to run the verb`.\n  The beam orch is an orch, not a task session, and it is the record's owner.\n- `lym board upsert --record-file` refuses an entry a run holds, and a beam's project is always held by its pawl run (`running · Beam-Await`), so that way is closed for the beam's whole life.\n\n## Expected\n\nThe beam's own orch can append a decision to, or replace, its beam's decision record while the beam lives, as a run's parent can for a task's record.\nThe caller check counts a beam orch (`\u003chub\u003e:\u003cbeam\u003e:orch`) as an orch, not as a task session.\n\n## Related\n\nThe same misclassification of the beam orch shows in parent-review: `lym loom parent-review answer` refuses the beam orch for its member's pull request (`neither the run's parent orch nor the strand the ask went to`), filed by the hub orch as Knatte18/loomyard#232.\n\n\n\n\n## From issue #252\n\nImported from [issue #252](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/252).\n\nA beam orch received a PR-Review pause notice for a task of its beam, naming 'lym tender resume \u003cslug\u003e' as the way forward. Run from the beam orch's session, 'lym tender resume \u003cslug\u003e' was refused: 'pause: an agent may pause, resume or revive only its own pair; way forward: ask your parent session'. The beam orch is the task's parent. Workaround: the prime orch ran the resume. Expected: the parent orch of a task, beam orch included, may resume its tasks' tender pauses, or the notice names a way forward open to it.\n\n## From issue #251\n\nImported from [issue #251](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/251).\n\nA task's PR-Review agent paused its own pair with lym tender pause before a push. The beam orch, which parents that task, then could not lift the pause: lym tender resume \u003ctask-slug\u003e from the beam orch's session is refused with 'an agent may pause, resume or revive only its own pair'. Mail from the beam orch to the paused agent is not delivered while the pause holds, so the run stays stuck until the prime orch runs the resume.\n\nExpected: the orch that parents a run may resume that run's pair, as it is the session that settles the run's halts.","owner":"neve","recipe":"darn","created":"2026-11-26T07:33:42Z","revised":"2026-11-26T12:30:49Z","design_file":"design-beam-member-parent.md","decision_file":"","revision":"ae45e91e54a4c12c74bc0dcfd4a56f29bff7672fd0261bd7cfa44fa5157ec2ba"},{"handle":"T23","slug":"fabric-core-push-origin","title":"Fabric core: push classification, retries, origin reconcile and failed-push records move to the core","kind":"task","types":["enhancement"],"areas":["fabric","testkit","shed","board","pattern"],"status":"","project":"P4","section":"Layer A","waits_on":["T11"],"brief":"","body":"Filed from the Sketch `fabric-git-free-core`.\n\nTakes: the foundation part's layout, core types, reader and executor loop\nDelivers: the rules of pushclassified, pushanchored, coalesce, failedpush, origin, originreads, originreconcile, originreconcileended, originreconcilemoves, pendingflags, pusherreads, pushlockholder and remoteonly in internal/fabriccore as phase and action-list rules over made-up snapshots, with the retry loop as wait actions and OriginSeams' teardown as a seam-call action; the engine running them, with rejectionRetryKind's error-to-class mapping and the live-pair probe before an origin move kept in the engine; these files' shedrun and shedengine imports cut: run paths, the prime run lock and the status codec reach fabric through required seams, the status readers return bytes their callers decode; D13's seam installer in internal/fabriccli, its call from cmd/lym/main.go and internal/hubforge's one-time call, each required seam's setter with its restore, the unset-seam refusal row and engine test, and shed's adapter with its test in the shed module; the D12 moves of this concern's boardkit and boardengine tests, such as pushclasses, pushanchored and originreconcile*, to internal/boardengine; PATTERN-fabric-boundary's background recorder clause restated as D4 words it, since this part's executor first records entries. Under the parent's added entry 'The restructure cuts fabric's test code, it does not only move it', this concern's tests shrink: a test is kept or moved only where it alone guards a rule, at the lowest tier that can check it, duplicates and the same rule tested at several tiers are deleted, not moved, and the change description lists every deleted test and, for each kept or moved test, the rule it alone guards\nExports: the required-seam installer and setters later parts add seams to; the push and origin core rules ReconcileOrigin callers reach through the unchanged public verbs\n\nThe part changes these packages:\n\n- internal/fabricengine (about 4100 changed lines)\n- internal/fabriccore (about 2500 changed lines)\n- internal/fabriclayout (about 200 changed lines)\n- internal/testkit/fabrickit (about 150 changed lines)\n- internal/fabriccli (about 250 changed lines)\n- internal/hubforge (about 20 changed lines)\n- cmd/lym (about 30 changed lines)\n- internal/shedengine (about 400 changed lines)\n- internal/boardengine (about 1200 changed lines)\n- pattern (about 20 changed lines)\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"tapestry","created":"2026-11-26T21:44:40Z","revised":"2026-11-27T09:43:18Z","design_file":"design-fabric-core-push-origin.md","decision_file":"decision-fabric-core-push-origin.md","revision":"464f714810bcdf6c7717d6f01415af4510107322ce46992f420c3d0f2e7b5e1a"},{"handle":"T24","slug":"fabric-core-teardown","title":"Fabric core: remove, destroy, cleanup, prune, remote teardown and pending teardown move to the core","kind":"task","types":["enhancement"],"areas":["fabric","testkit","shed","landing"],"status":"","project":"P4","section":"Layer B","waits_on":["T23"],"brief":"","body":"Filed from the Sketch `fabric-git-free-core`.\n\nTakes: the push and origin part's core rules, installer and setters\nDelivers: the rules of remove, cleanup, cleanupremotewarp, prune, archive, ownerarchive, remoteleftover, remoteteardown, pendingteardown and teardownlock in internal/fabriccore; the teardown child as a verb of its own that reads its own snapshot under the teardown lock, takes the handed facts and parent verdicts D2 lists, and pushes every archive tag before any origin delete as a core rule, with PushWithRetryReasons' loop kept inside one action; holdTeardown's in-process path reading the child's snapshot at the child's point; the child's entry point in internal/fabriccli taking the installed seams; destroy.go keeping its guards at execution with no rule moved into it; the pending-teardown record unchanged for fabric status, fabric prune and batten cancel; the D12 move of archiveparent's shed test; a test outside fabric that removes a hubforge pair unrefused with no seam of its own. Under the parent's added entry 'The restructure cuts fabric's test code, it does not only move it', this concern's tests, the heaviest of fabric's, shrink: a test is kept or moved only where it alone guards a rule, at the lowest tier that can check it, duplicates and the same rule tested at several tiers are deleted, not moved, and the change description lists every deleted test and, for each kept or moved test, the rule it alone guards\nExports: no new API: the teardown verbs keep their public signatures\n\nThe part changes these packages:\n\n- internal/fabricengine (about 3900 changed lines)\n- internal/fabriccore (about 3000 changed lines)\n- internal/fabriclayout (about 150 changed lines)\n- internal/testkit/fabrickit (about 150 changed lines)\n- internal/fabriccli (about 200 changed lines)\n- internal/shedengine (about 100 changed lines)\n- internal/pairteardown (about 150 changed lines)\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"tapestry","created":"2026-11-26T21:44:40Z","revised":"2026-11-26T21:44:40Z","design_file":"design-fabric-core-teardown.md","decision_file":"decision-fabric-core-teardown.md","revision":"3476fb50ee47d0430bff5de414ffbb651e3760359a5a72f6d2ed99e76711d73b"},{"handle":"T25","slug":"fabric-core-merge-landing","title":"Fabric core: merge-in, mid-merge, handover, takeover, integrate, task patch and discard move to the core","kind":"task","types":["enhancement"],"areas":["fabric","testkit","shed","batten","landing"],"status":"","project":"P4","section":"Layer C","waits_on":["T24"],"brief":"","body":"Filed from the Sketch `fabric-git-free-core`.\n\nTakes: the teardown part's tree, with the installer and the shed adapter\nDelivers: the rules of merge, mergeguards, mergelifecycle, mergestate, mergestateactive, mergestage, mergepaths, mergeerrors, midmerge, handover, takeover, takeoverwrite, integrateweft, taskpatch and taskbranchdiscard in internal/fabriccore; the shedengine.Status and shedengine.Takeover uses cut: OriginParentRunStatus and OriginBattenStatus return bytes and found, their callers decode through shed, ReadUpstreamTakeover and the result types that carried shedengine.Takeover carry fabric's own takeover entry of machine, subject and time filled by the seam's decoder, and the takeover write stays a state.WriteJSON patch of the one takeover key; the shed-module test written against today's code before the cut that pins the takeover write's status bytes, an undeclared key included, and the JSON-key parity of fabric's and shed's takeover types; the D12 moves of handover, takeoverrun and rejectedtakeover's shed tests to the shed module. Under the parent's added entry 'The restructure cuts fabric's test code, it does not only move it', this concern's tests shrink: a test is kept or moved only where it alone guards a rule, at the lowest tier that can check it, duplicates and the same rule tested at several tiers are deleted, not moved, and the change description lists every deleted test and, for each kept or moved test, the rule it alone guards\nExports: fabric's takeover entry type in internal/fabriclayout and the takeover decoder seam\n\nThe part changes these packages:\n\n- internal/fabricengine (about 3000 changed lines)\n- internal/fabriccore (about 2200 changed lines)\n- internal/fabriclayout (about 200 changed lines)\n- internal/testkit/fabrickit (about 100 changed lines)\n- internal/shedengine (about 900 changed lines)\n- internal/battencli (about 100 changed lines)\n- internal/landingshed (about 100 changed lines)\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"tapestry","created":"2026-11-26T21:44:40Z","revised":"2026-11-26T21:44:40Z","design_file":"design-fabric-core-merge-landing.md","decision_file":"decision-fabric-core-merge-landing.md","revision":"5a86589de788f09eb1965656a90b1ed5b84f781f1c921483e598afd6966b6799"},{"handle":"T26","slug":"fabric-core-pull-replay","title":"Fabric core: pull, replay, bolt, reconcile, parent tips and warp forward move to the core; stencil reads leave fabric","kind":"task","types":["enhancement"],"areas":["fabric","testkit","shed","stencil","board","heddle"],"status":"","project":"P4","section":"Layer D","waits_on":["T25"],"brief":"","body":"Filed from the Sketch `fabric-git-free-core`.\n\nTakes: the merge and landing part's tree, with the takeover entry type and the shed adapter\nDelivers: the rules of pull, hubpull, replay, bolt, boltreplay, reconcile, parenttips, codetipsettle, upstreamrun, stencilcommit and warpforward's fabric side in internal/fabriccore; the code-tip settle handing the seam the previous transition's status bytes with the status and lock paths, shed's adapter writing them through state.WriteJSON, and the shed-module test before the cut that pins its status bytes; upstreamrun's done verdict asked of the seam's decoder; replay's and pull's shedrun uses through the run-path seam; StencilSource and StencilBaseByStamp moved to internal/stencilsource (new) over fabricengine.HeadContains and the path-taking reads fabric adds (commit exists, ancestor of, a path's revisions, a file at a revision), with internal/stencilcli and cmd/lym's stencilseed retargeted and their two integration tests moved with them; the D12 moves of the bolt, pull and replay tests that drive boardengine, boardkit or heddleengine. Under the parent's added entry 'The restructure cuts fabric's test code, it does not only move it', this concern's tests shrink: a test is kept or moved only where it alone guards a rule, at the lowest tier that can check it, duplicates and the same rule tested at several tiers are deleted, not moved, and the change description lists every deleted test and, for each kept or moved test, the rule it alone guards\nExports: internal/stencilsource's StencilSource and StencilBaseByStamp; fabricengine's commit-exists, ancestor, path-revisions and file-at-revision reads\n\nThe part changes these packages:\n\n- internal/fabricengine (about 3200 changed lines)\n- internal/fabriccore (about 2200 changed lines)\n- internal/fabriclayout (about 150 changed lines)\n- internal/testkit/fabrickit (about 100 changed lines)\n- internal/stencilsource (about 700 changed lines)\n- internal/stencilcli (about 40 changed lines)\n- cmd/lym (about 30 changed lines)\n- internal/shedengine (about 400 changed lines)\n- internal/boardengine (about 900 changed lines)\n- internal/heddleengine (about 200 changed lines)\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"tapestry","created":"2026-11-26T21:44:40Z","revised":"2026-11-26T21:44:40Z","design_file":"design-fabric-core-pull-replay.md","decision_file":"decision-fabric-core-pull-replay.md","revision":"e15e15891f55dadb9e32b142443aba85f6e39417dc14235d7fc31fae768fe04b"},{"handle":"T27","slug":"fabric-core-add-clone","title":"Fabric core: add, clone, adopt, junctions, weft wiring and the hub workspace move to the core; the vscode import is cut","kind":"task","types":["enhancement"],"areas":["fabric","testkit","ide","batten","heddle","reconcile","shed"],"status":"","project":"P4","section":"Layer E","waits_on":["T26"],"brief":"","body":"Filed from the Sketch `fabric-git-free-core`.\n\nTakes: the pull and replay part's tree, with every earlier seam and the installer\nDelivers: the rules of add, addadopt, addadoptonly, addanchor, addparent, clone, checkout, checkoutstate, forkrecords, gitexclude, warpexclude, junction, junctionnames, pairbranch, pairparts, resetsubpathcheck, scratchlink, shortnamebinding, shortnamecommit, unwire, weftwiring and hubworkspace in internal/fabriccore; Add's rollback as the core's inverse action list over executed actions and pre-creation facts, the engine keeping createdToken and createdBranchToken for destroy.go, with a tier1 rollback on a real hub; add's, addanchor's and forkrecords' shedrun uses through the run-path seam; hubworkspace's vscode import cut by the workspace builder seam, installed by cmd/lym/main.go beside InstallPairFolderNamer and replacing InstallWorkspaceContent, called on every exit that regenerates the workspace today, a rollback included, with nil writing no file; the IDE module's builder and its test in internal/ideengine; builder installs in the tests outside fabric that read the hub workspace file, internal/ideengine/spawn_hub_integration_test.go and cmd/lym/prerunseams_test.go among them; internal/beamcli/dobbyleftover_integration_test.go dropping t.Parallel; the cmd/lym test that runs the binary on a hub with every seam installed; the D12 moves of the clone, worktreeseam and add_runrecords tests that drive fabriccli, heddleengine, hubreconcile, boardkit or shedrun. Under the parent's added entry 'The restructure cuts fabric's test code, it does not only move it', this concern's tests, the largest of fabric's, shrink: a test is kept or moved only where it alone guards a rule, at the lowest tier that can check it, duplicates and the same rule tested at several tiers are deleted, not moved, and the change description lists every deleted test and, for each kept or moved test, the rule it alone guards\nExports: fabricengine's workspace builder seam and its setter\n\nThe part changes these packages:\n\n- internal/fabricengine (about 4100 changed lines)\n- internal/fabriccore (about 2800 changed lines)\n- internal/fabriclayout (about 200 changed lines)\n- internal/testkit/fabrickit (about 150 changed lines)\n- internal/fabriccli (about 300 changed lines)\n- internal/ideengine (about 400 changed lines)\n- cmd/lym (about 150 changed lines)\n- internal/beamcli (about 10 changed lines)\n- internal/heddleengine (about 300 changed lines)\n- internal/hubreconcile (about 150 changed lines)\n- internal/shedrun (about 170 changed lines)\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"tapestry","created":"2026-11-26T21:44:40Z","revised":"2026-11-26T21:44:40Z","design_file":"design-fabric-core-add-clone.md","decision_file":"decision-fabric-core-add-clone.md","revision":"357d93b095fbb86877a439f775411546fbf46ff21daf6cad82c754b632e89a75"},{"handle":"T28","slug":"fabric-core-records-guards","title":"Fabric core: anchors, records store, commit, trailers and index move to the core; import guards, PATTERN close-out and remeasure","kind":"task","types":["enhancement"],"areas":["fabric","testkit","pattern","docs"],"status":"","project":"P4","section":"Layer F","waits_on":["T27"],"brief":"","body":"Filed from the Sketch `fabric-git-free-core`.\n\nTakes: the add and clone part's tree, every other concern moved\nDelivers: the rules of anchor, anchorchange, anchormarker, anchormove, anchorstop, anchortarget, commit, committedfile, commitweftpaths, index, moverecord, recordsgit, recordslayout, recordsprobe, recordsreads, snapshotuncommitted, trailer and trailercommit in internal/fabriccore, Commit's secret-path refusal as a pre-lock snapshot fact the engine fills from internal/secretpath's matcher; the cross-verb livestate, onerepository_lifecycle and droplocal tests and export_test.go reduced to what no other test guards; the tier0 guard on test imports of the three fabric packages (D12) and the tier0 guard on their production dependency closure (D13); PATTERN-fabric-boundary's rule line and the new PATTERN-fabric-core entry beside the guards they cite; the remeasure doc under docs/research/speed/, measured in one session against a checkout of the commit before the foundation part that the implementer asks its parent for, which also records fabric's test lines before and after. Under the parent's added entry 'The restructure cuts fabric's test code, it does not only move it', this concern's tests and the cross-verb tests shrink: a test is kept or moved only where it alone guards a rule, at the lowest tier that can check it, duplicates and the same rule tested at several tiers are deleted, not moved, and the change description lists every deleted test and, for each kept or moved test, the rule it alone guards\nExports: the two import guards\n\nThe part changes these packages:\n\n- internal/fabricengine (about 2200 changed lines)\n- internal/fabriccore (about 1800 changed lines)\n- internal/fabriclayout (about 150 changed lines)\n- internal/testkit/fabrickit (about 100 changed lines)\n- pattern (about 100 changed lines)\n- docs/research/speed (about 250 changed lines)\n\n## Added by the parent (2026-11-27)\n\nThe foundation part leaves temporary `testOnlyCodeAllowed` entries for chain functions whose first production caller is a later part (its record entry \"A chain part may leave production code that only a named later part first calls\").\nThis part's PATTERN close-out also states that allowance in `pattern/PATTERN-test-only-code.md` under `## Rule` for as long as such entries exist, or, once every entry is gone, checks that no entry of that kind is left and that the PATTERN text needs none.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"tapestry","created":"2026-11-26T21:44:40Z","revised":"2026-11-27T12:22:58Z","design_file":"design-fabric-core-records-guards.md","decision_file":"decision-fabric-core-records-guards.md","revision":"3e9bf5af6c28147545793f9413aa24b52e82febcfddd42e5e9dab36a22aadcc4"},{"handle":"T17","slug":"keep-landed-run-records","title":"Keep each landed run's findings, asks and fixer reports in its archive tag, with an index row and lym report landed","kind":"task","types":["feature"],"areas":["landing","fabric","report","loom","burler","shed","cwd","git","testkit","pattern"],"status":"","project":"P6","section":"Layer G","waits_on":["T11","T12","T28"],"brief":"","body":"Filed from the Sketch `keep-landed-records`.\n\nTakes: the Sketch's decision record whole: the archive tags teardown pushes, the loom status commit and run-records set, the parent-review ask record's locked updates in internal/burlerengine, the landing fixer and fix review scratch reports, and Finalize's merge-in, merge and push steps\nDelivers: one kept directory under each run's _lym/ that the status commit stages and the archive tag carries, holding PR findings copies, ask record and report copies seeded back after an adopt, landing fixer and fix review report copies, the landing record and the index row; and a read-only lym report landed verb that lists and extracts every archived run's kept records\nExports: the kept directory's name in internal/lymdirs; the landing record and index row types with their writers and one reader in internal/shedrun; the first-parent landing searches in internal/fabricengine; pullrequest.API.Merge returning the merge commit SHA; the cobra verb lym report landed\n\nThe part changes these packages:\n\n- internal/lymdirs (about 10 changed lines)\n- internal/loomengine (about 20 changed lines)\n- internal/loomcli (about 250 changed lines)\n- internal/fabricengine (about 200 changed lines)\n- internal/burlerengine (about 250 changed lines)\n- internal/landingshed (about 450 changed lines)\n- internal/pullrequest (about 30 changed lines)\n- internal/pairteardown (about 5 changed lines)\n- internal/testkit/prkit (about 5 changed lines)\n- internal/shedrun (about 140 changed lines)\n- internal/gitrepo (about 120 changed lines)\n- internal/reportengine (about 430 changed lines)\n- internal/reportcli (about 170 changed lines)\n- PATTERN.md (about 4 changed lines)\n- pattern (about 20 changed lines)\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"tapestry","created":"2026-11-26T11:58:21Z","revised":"2026-11-27T09:46:36Z","design_file":"design-keep-landed-run-records.md","decision_file":"decision-keep-landed-run-records.md","revision":"dff07a5db625fea0d95e926f6a2e3c1b58732dade5051b5fffc1b3019c542b99"},{"handle":"T21","slug":"hardener-study-faults","title":"Faults the hardener study read in fabric, cleanup and teardown code","kind":"task","types":["bug"],"areas":["fabric","cleanup","batten"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Layer G","waits_on":["T11","T28"],"brief":"","body":"## Seen\n\n`docs/research/hardener-works-study.md` (`c6618b538`), section \"Faults found by this study\", read these in the code; none was driven:\n\n- a refused credential during a one-repository seed keeps the hub and says origin may still take the push;\n- an empty teardown job clears an earlier teardown's pending record;\n- the `cleanup` remote sweep counts local tags as landed and never pushes them;\n- `--reset` is advised without regard to unpushed work;\n- `fetchRunForCatchUp` reads no records layout;\n- a `lym fabric remove` pending record can outlive its pair (`cd8090733` fixed only the cancel's records, by choice).\n\n## Idea\n\nConfirm each against the code with a test that fails first, and fix the confirmed ones.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"darn","created":"2026-11-26T11:25:44Z","revised":"2026-11-27T09:43:27Z","design_file":"design-hardener-study-faults.md","decision_file":"","revision":"b31fda1610bac64fc9f0966e4973ee057fb98913884683927b90202ba5488635"},{"handle":"T3","slug":"config-key-comment-sweep","title":"Name config keys as \u003cmodule\u003e.\u003cpath\u003e in Go comments, and guard string literals for bare keys","kind":"task","types":["enhancement"],"areas":["docs"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Layer H","waits_on":["T11","T21"],"brief":"","body":"After `config-keys-nested` nests the config keys by area, about 525 Go comment lines in about 199 files, plus single-word keys on top, still name a template key bare.\nPATTERN-config-key-names asks that such a comment say `\u003cmodule\u003e.\u003cpath\u003e`.\n\nSweep every Go comment and package doc that names a config key to its nested `\u003cmodule\u003e.\u003cpath\u003e` form.\nA comment-only change, no behaviour or user-visible text.\nDeferred from T3 code review round 1, finding F1, to keep that run short.\n\n\n## From issue #265\n\nImported from [issue #265](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/265).\n\n## What happened\n\nA code review finding asked for a repository-wide sweep of config key names written without their module, and no tool finds them.\nA grep over the underscored template keys was the only practical method.\nIt missed single-word keys such as `engine` and `scoper`, and any key that ended a string literal, so messages were reported done while several were not.\nSelf-check forks caught the misses.\n\nAn earlier implementer had also found bare key names in messages and help texts outside its card's packages:\n`gatecli`, `batcher`, `battencli`, `beamcli`, `webstercli`, `hibernatecli`, `reportcli`, `preflightshed`,\n`weaverengine` (`ResolveLadder` and `settingRefusal`), `sheddriver/tackler.go`, `shedarbiter`, `burlerengine/fix.go` and `loomshed/plancontext.go`.\nSome of these may have been fixed since.\n\n## Why it is abnormal\n\nA message or help text naming a key without its module can regress at any time, and nothing detects it mechanically.\n\n## What to do\n\nAdd a guard test that scans Go string literals for a config template's key names written without their module.\n\nProvenance: task `config-keys-nested`, notes `burler-code-r1.md`, `11-module-key-messages.md`.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"darn","created":"2026-11-26T13:52:00Z","revised":"2026-11-26T20:19:01Z","design_file":"design-config-key-comment-sweep.md","decision_file":"","revision":"a0bf01afc0964967a9a262d23ad0cc6bf07b5e9df0954616964ee43a3641bd79"},{"handle":"T7","slug":"area-creel-identity","title":"creel across an area: kernel-attached sender identity, the area registry and the hub directory","kind":"task","types":["feature"],"areas":["creel","cwd"],"status":"draft","project":"P7","section":"Draft","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"Filed from the Sketch `sandbox-closed-area-build`.\n\nTakes: the Sketch's decision record, D8 whole and the pieces of D3 and D6 that D8 rests on; peerPID (internal/creelengine/peer_linux.go), daemon.resolve and resolveSender, serveHubCall (receive.go), the outbound worker's spawn closure (outbound.go attempt), hubregistry's Entry, Lookup, Live and RepositoryName, creelcli's hubdirectory and its one machine-record write\nDelivers: every creel daemon sets SO_PASSCRED and SO_PASSPIDFD and takes the sender from SCM_CREDENTIALS and SCM_PIDFD with the pidfd liveness check; D3's inside test (barrier in /proc/\u003cpid\u003e/status or attr/current, plus a cgroup naming a scope of the hub's area by the whole-name forms lym-area-\u003chub key\u003e.scope and lym-area-\u003chub key\u003e-\u003cpane|run|attach\u003e-\u003cpid\u003e.scope), with which a daemon serves only deliver and confirm to a peer inside another hub's area or a peer gone or unreadable; the machine record moved to the hub directory \u003cper-user lym state dir\u003e/hubs/ with its lock, read under a shared lock on a read-only descriptor, with the old hubs.json read for entries the new record lacks; each daemon writing its shortname, creel socket path, repository name and the area-aware creel level into its entry, bar an in-area daemon; the area registry area-hubs.json and its lock in the hub directory, its reader, and a writer the core part's lym area start calls; hubregistry.Lookup, the confirm's geometry and RepositoryName reading an area hub's registry entry and, inside an area, every other hub's host-written record, never a Bolt shortname file or a prime's .git/config; every registry reader and writer dropping an entry whose area file ~/.config/lym/area-\u003chub key\u003e.yaml is gone (Added after Discussion); the outbound worker starting no other hub's daemon inside an area, and a hub without an area starting no area hub's daemon; PATTERN-agent-addressing's Across hubs background gaining the area-registry repository rule\nExports: areaengine.HubKey (reedengine.ServerName of the canonical hub path), areaengine.FilePath(hubKey), areaengine.ScopeName and the scope-name parser, areaengine.Inside(pid, hubKey, barrier); hubregistry.AreaEntry {hub_path, shortname, creel_socket, repository}, hubregistry.ReadAreaRegistry and hubregistry.WriteAreaEntry, the hub-directory path helper; the area-aware creel level constant D5 admits\n\nThe part changes these packages:\n\n- internal/creelengine (about 450 changed lines)\n- internal/hubregistry (about 350 changed lines)\n- internal/creelcli (about 150 changed lines)\n- internal/areaengine (about 250 changed lines)\n- internal/lymdirs (about 20 changed lines)\n- internal/machinecli (about 30 changed lines)\n- internal/slackcli (about 20 changed lines)\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"darn","created":"2026-11-26T11:58:18Z","revised":"2026-11-26T11:58:18Z","design_file":"design-area-creel-identity.md","decision_file":"decision-area-creel-identity.md","revision":"4d56faff3481a8de1737a1276c5f4a0babbea1a7acc787ab152a3389b5c0712b"},{"handle":"T14","slug":"area-seccomp-core","title":"Stage 1 of the closed area: supervisor, Landlock ruleset, connect filter, attach client and the in-area engines, token and relay","kind":"task","types":["feature"],"areas":["reed","shuttle","claude","codex","creel","dobby","slack","process","cwd","git","cli","docs","pattern"],"status":"draft","project":"P7","section":"Draft","waits_on":["T7","T11"],"brief":"","body":"Filed from the Sketch `sandbox-closed-area-build`.\n\nTakes: the creel identity part's exports (inside test, scope names, area registry writer, hub directory, area-aware level); config-keys-nested's rewritten PATTERN-config-layers line; docs/research/platform/seccomp-connect-probe/main.go as D4's starting point; reedengine's server spawn and scrub (lifecycle.go), reedcli's attach (EnsureSession, Status, the watchdog spawn), proc.CapProcessMemory and RunMemoryCapped, lymtemp's root and run directories, runproc's end verbs, claudeengine's account resolution, TranscriptReader and user-setup source, codexengine's homes and links, githubclient's token chain, slackengine's EnsureRelay, StopRelay and file geometry, creelcli's SendReady\nDelivers: lym area init, start, stop, show, supervise, exec, git-credential and slack-setup; the area file outside the hub and its write-grant refusals; the supervisor in its own scope with its record, socket, SO_PEERCRED check, subreaping and verbs boot, attach-scope, cap-memory, kill-scope and slack-relay (start, stop, presence, status); the one launcher setting no_new_privs, Landlock ABI 8 with TSYNC and the filter with TSYNC, TSYNC_ESRCH and NEW_LISTENER; the connect decision on the checked inode and the derived allow list; the per-hub area root under lymtemp; the host-side attach client under the area; lym area start writing the machine record and the registry entry for an area hub; the host-side refusal of D7 as the first root pre-run step and at every hub selection, with machine walks skipping area hubs; dobby and creel spawn sites refusing from outside; the memory caps through the supervisor; the area's own engine config directories, GitHub token and relay records; README.md, docs/overview.md and the glossary per D16, the pseudo-terminal bound included (Added after Discussion); PATTERN-closed-area added, PATTERN-child-processes changed, and the backgrounds of PATTERN-child-processes, PATTERN-lym-files and PATTERN-config-layers per the notes for the plan writer; the seccomp half of the under-each-barrier criteria, whose apparmor half the fallback part carries; and the D6 attach criterion's second clause, which the record's unindented continuation line cuts from the parsed bullet: a `detach-client -E` sent to the attach client runs its command under the area's filter and in the attach scope\nExports: the supervisor's verb protocol and its client in internal/areaengine (one request per connection, the verbs named above), the launcher's start-request protocol, the area file type and its loader, the barrier enum seccomp and apparmor, and the start check's hook for a barrier-specific refusal, on which the fallback and host-exit parts add verbs and a barrier\n\nThe part changes these packages:\n\n- internal/areaengine (about 3000 changed lines)\n- internal/areacli (about 500 changed lines)\n- cmd/lym (about 150 changed lines)\n- internal/reedengine (about 250 changed lines)\n- internal/reedcli (about 400 changed lines)\n- internal/proc (about 250 changed lines)\n- internal/lymtemp (about 120 changed lines)\n- internal/runproc (about 40 changed lines)\n- internal/dobbyengine (about 40 changed lines)\n- internal/creelcli (about 80 changed lines)\n- internal/machinecli (about 80 changed lines)\n- internal/hubregistry (about 120 changed lines)\n- internal/shuttleengine/claudeengine (about 250 changed lines)\n- internal/shuttleengine/codexengine (about 200 changed lines)\n- internal/githubclient (about 120 changed lines)\n- internal/slackengine (about 350 changed lines)\n- internal/slackcli (about 120 changed lines)\n- internal/hubgeom (about 80 changed lines)\n- README.md (about 80 changed lines)\n- docs (about 80 changed lines)\n- pattern (about 40 changed lines)\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"tapestry","created":"2026-11-26T11:58:18Z","revised":"2026-11-26T12:03:20Z","design_file":"design-area-seccomp-core.md","decision_file":"decision-area-seccomp-core.md","revision":"da1dd7d0d337e30689b0daa5d0a26e051eb1cc99695b49a9d287f6f668b86883"},{"handle":"T15","slug":"area-apparmor-barrier","title":"The closed area's AppArmor fallback barrier, built only when the OSL-1046 probe fails","kind":"task","types":["feature"],"areas":["process"],"status":"draft","project":"P7","section":"Draft","waits_on":["T14"],"brief":"","body":"Filed from the Sketch `sandbox-closed-area-build`.\n\nTakes: the core part's supervisor, launcher, attach client, area file with its barrier key, and the start check's barrier hook; the OSL-1046 probe result of D14 step 1\nDelivers: lym area profile rendering the hub's profile from the area file's lists and the Landlock paths, with other hubs' creel sockets as one creel-*.sock pattern and the name a hash of the text, and naming the root load command; the launcher and attach client entering it through aa-exec, each still installing a filter with D4's foreign-ABI, io_uring_setup and clone3 rules, no connect rule, no listener, and TSYNC with TSYNC_ESRCH (Added after Discussion); the start refusing while no loaded profile carries the rendered name; the inside test's apparmor label branch exercised end to end\nExports: areaengine's apparmor barrier and its profile renderer\n\nThe part changes these packages:\n\n- internal/areaengine (about 350 changed lines)\n- internal/areacli (about 100 changed lines)\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"darn","created":"2026-11-26T11:58:18Z","revised":"2026-11-26T11:58:18Z","design_file":"design-area-apparmor-barrier.md","decision_file":"decision-area-apparmor-barrier.md","revision":"2cd8c22abd358be34dc337618b3e0a9099c807378e35e79a040f22ae5bd411ac"},{"handle":"T16","slug":"area-host-exits","title":"Stage 2 of the closed area: window, hibernate and deploy as supervisor verbs, and lym ide refused inside","kind":"task","types":["feature"],"areas":["termwindow","hibernate","batten","ide","deploy","reed"],"status":"draft","project":"P7","section":"Draft","waits_on":["T15"],"brief":"","body":"Filed from the Sketch `sandbox-closed-area-build`.\n\nTakes: the core part's supervisor verb protocol and attach client; termwindow's Launcher.OpenAttach, hibernatecli's request and its systemctl spawn, battenshed's LandDeps.RunDeploy and its wiring, idecli; the operator's stop of D14 step 3\nDelivers: the supervisor's window verb (direct child of the hub root, title built from the directory name, Konsole running the host's lym reed attach with no shell after it) with lym reed window routed to it inside an area; the hibernate verb running systemctl hibernate alone; the deploy verb recording a pending deploy from Batten's Land row with a notice; lym area deploy cloning https://github.com/\u003crepository\u003e.git into the area state directory, fetching the area file's deploy_branch, checking the commit is on it and running the area file's deploy command with the host's toolchain and caches, refusing with the way forward when repository, deploy or deploy_branch is missing, and lym area init writing deploy_branch as main (Added after Discussion, with its acceptance criterion that a rewritten origin and a hub record naming another branch change neither); lym ide refusing inside an area\nExports: the window, hibernate and deploy verbs and their clients\n\nThe part changes these packages:\n\n- internal/areaengine (about 300 changed lines)\n- internal/areacli (about 200 changed lines)\n- internal/termwindow (about 50 changed lines)\n- internal/reedcli (about 40 changed lines)\n- internal/hibernatecli (about 40 changed lines)\n- internal/battenshed (about 60 changed lines)\n- internal/battencli (about 40 changed lines)\n- internal/idecli (about 20 changed lines)\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"darn","created":"2026-11-26T11:58:18Z","revised":"2026-11-26T11:58:18Z","design_file":"design-area-host-exits.md","decision_file":"decision-area-host-exits.md","revision":"a3b15d5602b78c496bf04602933ffdee0cea5c686924e38df257f3c0e44152c4"},{"handle":"B1","slug":"webster-audit-old-friction-note","title":"Webster run-exit audit flags a resumed Master earlier friction note","kind":"note","types":["bug"],"areas":["webster"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Bugs","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"Seen on weaver-bench-b2 (2026-11-27): Webster Master's first prompt named its friction note `_lym/loom/friction/webster-master.md`, and Master wrote it.\nAfter `lym loom resume` the new Master prompt named the note `webster-master-2.md`.\nThe run-exit audit then flagged the earlier, legitimate note as a parent-write and blocked the Webster row, although the audit itself reported the path as cleared, unchanged since Master spawned.\nThe orch had to run `lym webster accept-audit` by hand.\n\nIdea: the audit treats every friction note path a Master of this run was given as Master's own, not only the latest prompt's.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-27T13:29:08Z","revised":"2026-11-27T13:29:08Z","design_file":"design-webster-audit-old-friction-note.md","decision_file":"","revision":"26d25acb8c94b75457ee1ed76162c68763a6be390ff7f84f8cd6d8ade380f359"},{"handle":"B2","slug":"upkeep-gate-lacks-tier1","title":"Upkeep gate call is refused because gate.gates.upkeep lists no tier covering tier1","kind":"note","types":["bug"],"areas":["gate","config"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Bugs","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"## Seen (board tidy, 2026-11-27, main at `2326ef4da`)\n\n`lym gate test --gate upkeep` was refused, so it ran no check:\n\n```\ngate test: --gate \"upkeep\" lists no tier that is or covers tier1, so its marked tests there cannot run: TestCSharpFixtures_CompileWithDotnet, TestGateRedFirst_Dotnet, TestGateTest_DotnetFixture, TestTestOnlyCode_EveryProductionFunctionIsReachableFromAMain; set gate.gates.upkeep to list tier1; way forward: fix it with \"lym config gate\" from the prime; a session that is not the prime takes none of these steps; it reports status: FAILED and the orch runs them\n```\n\n## Fix\n\nSet `gate.gates.upkeep` to `{tiers: [0, 1], scope: marked}` with `lym config gate` from the prime, as the change description of `2326ef4da` asks under \"To check by hand\".\nEvery binary that reads the hub's gate config must hold that change first, since an older one does not accept the scope `marked`.\nThen run `lym gate test --gate upkeep` on the main branch and confirm that it runs the dead-code and .NET tests.\n\nThe shipped template gives `{tiers: [0], scope: marked}` while the marked upkeep tests are `tier1`,\nso a hub whose `tier0` does not cover `tier1` is refused the same way until it sets the key.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-27T14:35:03Z","revised":"2026-11-27T14:35:03Z","design_file":"design-upkeep-gate-lacks-tier1.md","decision_file":"","revision":"71a137e20d30904408e8397bf7bb1b6cc8be63bc02e85520521b84055e6d2479"},{"handle":"E4","slug":"landing-queue","title":"A landing queue gates several ready runs as one batch and lands them together","kind":"note","types":["enhancement"],"areas":["landing","gate","batten"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Enhancements","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"## Seen (operator, 2026-11-27)\n\nEach landing ran its own landing gate under the parent lock, one after the other, so with many runs ready the gates queued in series.\n\n## Since (`d87a9ce24`, `landing-lock-only-for-land`)\n\n`Finalize` now merges the parent's tip and gates the merged tree with no parent lock held, and takes the lock only to land.\nReady runs therefore gate side by side.\nA run whose parent moved during its gate merges the new tip and gates again,\nand after `landing.finalize_lost_races` lost races (default 2) it merges and gates under the lock.\nEach ready run still pays for its own gate, plus one for each race it loses.\n\n## Idea\n\nA landing queue in the manner of GitHub's merge queue, bors or Zuul:\n\n- A run ready to land joins a queue for its parent branch instead of gating and taking the parent lock itself.\n- The queue takes the waiting runs, say A, B and C, builds the tree parent + A + B + C and runs the landing gate once on it.\n- A pass lands all of them together.\n- A failure is bisected (parent + A + B, and so on): the runs that pass land, and the culprit goes back to its own run's fixer.\n- A later step may gate the prefixes in parallel (parent + A, parent + A + B, parent + A + B + C) so a failure costs little time.\n\nNo drift: the batch is gated exactly as it lands.\nIt saves the most gate time when many runs land often, and it costs the most to build:\na queue service, batch merging, bisection on failure and fixer handling for the culprit.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-27T07:03:20Z","revised":"2026-11-27T14:34:50Z","design_file":"design-landing-queue.md","decision_file":"","revision":"eab67b787b4b4d20948e49afe2e05b0757ed12fb26324200c4b7dca3d3f7bd59"},{"handle":"F17","slug":"orchestrator-survey-ideas","title":"Remaining leads from the survey of comparable agent orchestrators (Oct 2026)","kind":"note","types":["feature"],"areas":["burler","shed","orch","quarry","report"],"status":"","project":"P2","section":"Features","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"Leads borrowed from comparable open-source agent orchestrators, surveyed in October 2026 (first imported from issue #671).\nNothing here is a commitment; these are leads to triage.\nThe orch checked each against the code on 2026-11-17: ideas lym has built or that proved moot are dropped,\npartly built ones name only what is still open, and the eight most worth doing are notes of their own in this project.\nEach bullet names its source project and the lym module it would touch.\n\n## Plan validation and concurrency (quarry, Plan-Write, board)\n\n- **Typed scope operations on cards** (foremerge): each scope carries an additive or breaking operation,\n  and deterministic rules flag colliding pairs even when files do not overlap.\n  Worth comparing against board labels and the alignment gate.\n- **Symbol leases between concurrent tasks** (Fusion, ORCH): expiring leases on symbol keys serialize overlapping tasks,\n  with a deterministic tie-break; lym keeps overlapping tasks apart by board labels.\n- **Commit-time reservation guard** (ntm): a pre-commit hook refuses a commit touching what another task holds; needs leases first.\n- **Criterion amendment ledger** (oh-my-claudecode): a card's acceptance text changes only through a recorded amendment in weft.\n\n## Review (burler, Bouncer)\n\n- **Quorum of differently-angled judges** (ccswarm): lym fans out lenses but has no quorum vote for high-risk segments.\n  Since 2026-11-19 a run's parent orch can be asked to see and veto each Code-Review round and each drift judge ruling\n  (`loom.code.review_ask_parent`, `drift.judge_ask_parent`); that is one extra judge, not a quorum.\n- **Risk tier by changed paths** (yylo): globs over sensitive paths choose the review depth and the gate scope.\n\n## Engine, recipes and governance (shed, tender, orch, board)\n\n- **Cross-run quarantine** (Bernstein): a task that fails three times across runs is held, unless the failures were the host's.\n- **Fork a run from a step with a tree snapshot** (Bernstein): `shed goto` moves the cursor but does not restore the warp tree.\n- **Warm retry guarded by a workspace hash** (Bernstein): resume the native session only when the tree is unchanged.\n  Partly met since `dfade5f9a`: a run agent whose session a crash, reboot or killed pane ended resumes it (engines with a `SessionResumer`, so not Codex);\n  no tree hash guards that resume, and a retry after a timeout or a finished run still starts fresh.\n  Since `f1311f21d` a row gate also replaces a cold gate fixer that a machine stop or a killed pane interrupted,\n  resuming its session where shuttle can; no tree hash guards that either.\n- **Evidence reuse by input identity** (yylo): verify is skipped on the same tree; a changed review prompt could rerun only the review.\n- **Guidance at the next step boundary** (Conductor): a queue of operator guidance for the next producer's prompt.\n  Partly met since 2026-11-19: `loom.notify_parent_on` mails the run's parent when a named row is reached,\n  so the parent can brief that row's agent as it starts; there is still no queue of guidance lym itself folds into the prompt.\n- **Batched operator questions with recorded assumptions** (Spec Kit `/clarify`, Conductor): Discussion-Write asks at most a few ranked questions,\n  each with a recommended answer, and records unanswered ones as assumptions.\n  Since 2026-11-19 a run's autonomous agents put their questions to the run's parent orch, not to the operator in their pane.\n  Discussion asks in its pane only when `loom.discussion.interactive` is `true`, which is off by default and forced off by autonomy mode (`30ed1ee58`),\n  so this lead concerns an interactive Discussion only.\n- **Rate-limit wait**: the orch watcher wakes the orch after a usage reset; tender itself schedules no resume.\n- **Standing goals and scheduled routines** (ORCH, CAO, Fusion): periodic triage, dependency bumps or `selfreport` sweeps.\n\n## Agent sessions (reed, shuttle)\n\n- **Waiting-for-input signal** (agent-of-empires): shuttle has an awaiting-input state; screen heuristics still decide readiness and startup.\n- **Reference status patterns and a paste pitfall** (CAO): compare its regexes and its tmux bracketed-paste note with shuttle's readiness and creel inject.\n- **Context forecasting** (ntm): the orch cycles its context; Webster's Master and the loom driver do not, and nothing forecasts.\n- **Staggered spawns** (ntm): space Webster forks and batten fan-out by learned rate limits.\n\n## Observability (report, weft)\n\n- **Hash-chained run journal** (Bernstein): a hash chain over producer outputs makes an archived run self-verifying.\n- **Unverified-completion rate** (Bernstein): a `lym report` view of completions with no test or gate behind them.\n- **Commit-to-record links** (AgentNote): weft links to warp by a `Warp-SHA:` trailer; a note or trailer on each warp commit could link back.\n- **Run diff** (ccswarm): `lym report` comparing two archived runs' timelines and token use.\n- **OpenTelemetry spans per producer** (Conductor).\n\n## Operator attention (orch, dobby, reed)\n\n- **Attention feed with acknowledgement states** (ntm): dobby's wait reminders have no seen, snoozed or acknowledged states.\n\n## Discussion review (Discussion-Write, stencils, pattern)\n\n- **Requirements-quality checklist** (Spec Kit `/checklist`): items with IDs the Discussion-review judge answers one by one.\n- **Fixed analysis categories** (Kiro): inconsistency, ambiguity, conflicts, unstated assumptions and missing edge cases as named lenses.\n- **PATTERN rules handed to reviewers** (Spec Kit constitution): its rules as numbered MUSTs in plan and code review prompts.\n- **Glob-scoped steering** (Kiro): stencils pull per-area guidance chosen by the paths of a plan's glyphs.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-10-16T06:52:07Z","revised":"2026-11-27T07:31:40Z","design_file":"design-orchestrator-survey-ideas.md","decision_file":"","revision":"45340d3f7ceab7c6c0ba773eca929ea01c976c2fb2ed6a0fb636c64ff4456713"},{"handle":"R4","slug":"antigravity-engine-spike","title":"Antigravity CLI engine spike","kind":"note","types":["research"],"areas":["shuttle"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Research","waits_on":[],"brief":"Hands-on spike of agy in a tmux pane: screens, hooks, transcript, login","body":"## Seen (orch, 2026-11-17)\n\nThe desk study `docs/research/gemini-antigravity-engine-study-2026-11-17.md` (`246c9146`) judged Antigravity CLI (`agy`) the better Google engine target.\nIts terms aim at third-party services reselling access, not at the operator driving the CLI with a tool they wrote, as with Codex and Claude (operator, 2026-11-17).\nSeveral of its readings are undocumented and can only be learnt hands-on.\n\n## Idea\n\nA hands-on spike like Codex's of 2026-10-24, run as darn docs, that installs `agy` and runs it in a tmux pane on Neve.\nIt answers the study's open points:\n\n- the busy, idle, trust and interrupt screens, and bracketed paste plus Enter through `paste-buffer -p`;\n- whether the keyring login works in a lym pane;\n- the hook payloads for SessionStart, PreToolUse, PostToolUse, PostInvocation and Stop (`continue`, `fullyIdle`);\n- the transcript's on-disk layout and token fields;\n- whether a conversation id can be chosen up front, and whether the `self` subagent inherits the caller's history.\n\nCap: one machine, one model, one short session per point, no lym code changed.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-17T18:54:35Z","revised":"2026-11-17T18:54:35Z","design_file":"design-antigravity-engine-spike.md","decision_file":"","revision":"050ddc1b61f6b055aa9d9b6c628533fe44d2d55b1714f47bce2bcd427506c7b5"}]};
